1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,340
Have you ever contemplated what the true nature is of the attachments you cling to so tightly and the realities you take for granted?

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:15,120
Today, I will lead everyone to deeply experience one of the most profound wisdoms in Buddhism—the emptiness of all phenomena.

3
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:25,080
This is not only the truth directly proclaimed by the Buddha over two thousand five hundred years ago, but also a beacon of wisdom that transcends time and space, capable of illuminating our modern lives.

4
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:32,340
The phrase "all dharmas are empty in nature," though seemingly simple on the surface, contains the key to unraveling the truth of life.

5
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,680
It does not teach us nihilism, the belief that nothing exists, but rather reveals an even more astonishing truth.

6
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:48,100
All that we cling to—material possessions, emotions, thoughts, even the very sense of self—are not as solid, unchanging, or independently existent as we imagine.

7
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:55,060
When you truly understand the meaning of emptiness, you will realize that it does not take anything away, but rather grants infinite possibilities.

8
00:00:56,140 --> 00:01:01,060
This is a form of Middle Way wisdom that transcends existence and non-existence, birth and death, gain and loss.

9
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:07,180
Dear friend with a karmic connection, it is no coincidence that you are seeing this video and encountering this wisdom at this moment.

10
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:14,560
If this wisdom of the Dharma brings you joy and bliss, please write in the comment section that it originally came from Shakyamuni Buddha.

11
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:18,640
This is both a blessing and an affirmation of the Buddha's wisdom.

12
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:22,680
The concept of the emptiness of all dharmas does not arise out of thin air.

13
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:25,060
It originates from the Buddha's realization under the Bodhi tree.

14
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:30,980
It originates from the tranquil night when the Buddha attained enlightenment beneath the Bodhi tree, revealing profound truths to humanity after his insight into the nature of the world.

15
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:38,080
Among the teachings of the Buddha, the most direct scripture elucidating emptiness is the Heart Sutra of Prajnaparamita.

16
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:53,080
The sutra contains a widely known passage: "Form does not differ from emptiness; emptiness does not differ from form. Form is emptiness; emptiness is form." These sixteen characters, brief yet profound, have become a crystallization of wisdom spanning millennia, guiding countless practitioners toward the gate of liberation.

17
00:01:53,360 --> 00:02:00,240
It is said that during the time of the Buddha, a disciple named Kātyāyana once asked the Buddha about the question of being and non-being.

18
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:06,440
Sentient beings often fall into dualistic thinking, believing that the world either exists or does not exist.

19
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:12,480
The Buddha told Kātyāyana that if a person sees existence, they are one-sided; if they see non-existence, they are also one-sided.

20
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:19,920
The Tathāgata’s teachings abide in the Middle Way, which is the origin of the famous Madhyamaka philosophy and the key to understanding emptiness.

21
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:26,860
As the Dharma spread, the concept of emptiness received richer interpretations across different periods and regions.

22
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:32,720
In India, Bodhisattva Nagarjuna expounded the concept of emptiness through the Middle Way (Madhyamaka) and dependent origination (pratītyasamutpāda).

23
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:48,020
When the Dharma was transmitted eastward to China, the Sixth Patriarch Huineng's teaching of "originally there is not a single thing; where then can dust alight?" integrated the wisdom of emptiness into the sudden enlightenment method of Chan Buddhism. In contrast, Tibetan Buddhism advanced the practice of emptiness to new heights through the Mahāmadhyamaka (Great Middle Way) philosophical framework.

24
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:53,020
There is an ancient Zen koan that can help us understand the meaning of emptiness.

25
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:57,020
A monk asked Master Fayan, "What is the True Dharma Realm?"

26
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:10,600
The Dharma Eye responds: "Ten thousand miles become rice; the same village becomes water." This seemingly plain and unremarkable answer directly points to the suchness of phenomena. Things are just as they are, neither increasing nor decreasing, with no fixed, unchanging essence to cling to.

27
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:29,280
It is noteworthy that although the concept of emptiness has taken various forms throughout the history of Buddhist development, its core meaning has remained consistent: all phenomena lack inherent, independent, and permanent existence. This understanding dispels delusions and attachments to the world of appearances, guiding sentient beings towards liberation.

28
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:42,040
When we stand in the 21st century and look back upon this ancient wisdom, we find that it has not faded with the passage of time; rather, it shines even more brilliantly when illuminated by modern science and philosophy.

29
00:03:42,360 --> 00:04:00,360
Modern physics' quantum theory, molecular biology's exploration of the nature of life, and psychology's study of the self-concept all corroborate the Buddha's ancient teachings from different perspectives: there is no independent, permanent, unchanging entity; all phenomena arise, exist, and cease in mutual dependence and interaction.

30
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:10,460
This ancient and timeless wisdom transcends cultural and temporal boundaries, serving as a vital key to interpreting the human condition and seeking inner peace.

31
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:18,300
Now, let us delve deeper into the core significance of the emptiness of all dharmas, unveiling the mysterious veil of this treasure of wisdom.

32
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:24,000
To understand the emptiness of all dharmas, we must first grasp the true meaning of these four characters.

33
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:36,640
Dharmas refer to all phenomena in the world, encompassing not only the material world that we can see and touch, but also our thoughts, emotions, memories, as well as more abstract concepts and ideas.

34
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:46,000
From the microscopic atoms and molecules to the macroscopic galaxies and universe, from the existence of matter to the activities of the mind, all fall within the category of dharmas.

35
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:51,400
The emptiness within emptiness is one of the most easily misunderstood concepts in Buddhism.

36
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:55,140
Many people mistakenly believe that emptiness means nothingness or non-existence.

37
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,420
This is a severe distortion of the Dharma.

38
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:03,820
The true meaning of emptiness is the lack of intrinsic nature, that is, the absence of an independent, permanent, and unchanging essence.

39
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:07,200
"Rūpa" refers to the form, which is the outward appearance or characteristic of phenomena.

40
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:14,340
Therefore, the emptiness of all dharmas teaches us that behind the appearance of all phenomena there is a lack of inherent and unchanging essence.

41
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:17,020
Let me explain with a simple example.

42
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:24,220
Imagine the smartphone in your hand; on the surface, it appears to be an independently existing physical object.

43
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:30,060
However, upon deeper contemplation, we realize that the existence of this mobile phone depends on countless conditions.

44
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:37,400
Metal, glass, plastic and other material substances, the design by engineers, the production by factories, as well as the support of the power system.

45
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:42,740
If you were to disassemble this mobile phone, you would find that it is composed of thousands of individual components.

46
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,180
And each component is further composed of more fundamental elements.

47
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,760
These elements are composed of atoms.

48
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:51,840
Atoms also contain protons, neutrons, and electrons.

49
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:56,360
Ultimately, you will find that there is nothing that can be called an inherently existent essence.

50
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:00,860
This mobile phone is merely the product of numerous causes and conditions temporarily coming together.

51
00:06:00,980 --> 00:06:03,380
It itself does not possess an inherent, unchanging nature.

52
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:06,580
The same principle also applies to ourselves.

53
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:10,060
We habitually conceive of a fixed and unchanging self.

54
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:17,880
However, the Dharma teaches us that this self is merely a temporary aggregation of the five skandhas: form, feeling, perception, volition, and consciousness.

55
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:22,520
Our bodies are continuously undergoing metabolism, with cells constantly being born and dying.

56
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:27,120
Our emotions and thoughts also continuously change according to the environment and conditions.

57
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:32,400
Upon deep observation, you will find that there is no permanent and unchanging self.

58
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:36,340
This is the wisdom of no-self, which is the key to understanding emptiness.

59
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:47,060
However, please note that "no-self" does not mean the self does not exist; rather, it means the self is not an independent, fixed, and unchanging entity, but a composite temporarily manifested depending on various conditions.

60
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:53,680
An important concept in Dharma is "pratītyasamutpāda" or dependent origination, which refers to the understanding that all phenomena arise due to the coming together of causes and conditions.

61
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:54,300
Dependent origination arises from the conjunction of causes and conditions.

62
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:57,080
Dependent origination and emptiness are both fundamental truths.

63
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:01,560
Precisely because all phenomena arise dependent on causes and conditions, they are empty of inherent existence.

64
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:04,940
Precisely because they are empty in nature, they are able to arise dependent on conditions.

65
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:08,160
This is not a contradiction but a truth of two aspects of one entity.

66
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:16,480
Imagine a river; Heraclitus said that one cannot step into the same river twice because the water is constantly flowing and changing.

67
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:25,560
A river appears to be a continuous, enduring entity, but in reality, it is changing every moment and does not possess a fixed, unchanging essence.

68
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:33,020
It is precisely because the river lacks a fixed and unchanging essence—emptiness—that it can continuously flow and change, arising dependently.

69
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:39,600
Precisely because it is in constant flux and change, arising dependent on conditions, it has no fixed or unchanging essence—this is emptiness (śūnyatā).

70
00:07:39,860 --> 00:07:44,460
After understanding the meaning of emptiness, we need to clarify that śūnyatā (emptiness) is not nihilism.

71
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:54,900
Emptiness in Buddhism does not mean that things do not exist; rather, it means that things do not exist in the way we imagine. They lack an independent, permanent, and unchanging essence.

72
00:07:55,140 --> 00:08:01,420
This understanding breaks our rigid perceptions of the world and opens a completely new perspective for viewing it.

73
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:11,000
But I must tell you an even deeper truth, which is also a common trap for many practitioners of Buddhism: attachment to emptiness itself is also a form of attachment.

74
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:28,020
The Buddha said in the Diamond Sutra that if a Bodhisattva has the notions of self, person, living being, or lifespan, he is not a true Bodhisattva. Similarly, if we become attached to the concept of emptiness, treating emptiness as some kind of entity or absolute truth, we fall into another form of attachment.

75
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:35,500
True wisdom neither clings to existence nor to non-existence; it transcends duality and returns to the Middle Way.

76
00:08:35,780 --> 00:08:43,340
This leads to a seemingly paradoxical yet profound statement: form is emptiness, emptiness is form, encompassing all phenomena of the world.

77
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:49,300
The essence of form is emptiness, yet emptiness does not exist independently apart from all phenomena in the world.

78
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:54,620
Emptiness manifests through all phenomena in the world, and all phenomena exist through emptiness.

79
00:08:54,920 --> 00:09:02,380
This is a kind of wisdom that transcends language and concepts, attainable only through deep contemplation and meditation.

80
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,620
The wisdom of emptiness is also manifested in a profound understanding of impermanence.

81
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:16,860
Impermanence is one of the Three Marks of Existence in Buddhism, referring to the fact that all conditioned phenomena, which arise from causes and conditions, are in a constant state of flux and possess no eternal, unchanging essence.

82
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:23,660
From the microscopic perspective, science tells us that within atoms, which constitute matter, electrons are in constant motion.

83
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:28,680
From a macroscopic perspective, planets rotate on their axes and revolve around stars, while the universe is expanding.

84
00:09:28,820 --> 00:09:32,860
From the perspective of human society, dynasties succeed one another and civilizations rise and fall.

85
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:39,340
From the perspective of individual life, we undergo birth, aging, sickness, and death, as well as fluctuations in emotions and changes in thoughts.

86
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:44,720
The ubiquitous nature of change teaches us that grasping anything is like trying to hold flowing water.

87
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:49,380
I often use the ocean and its waves as a metaphor for the relationship between emptiness and phenomena.

88
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:54,100
Waves appear to be independent entities, some high and some low, some large and some small.

89
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:57,720
But essentially, they are all temporary forms of seawater.

90
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:02,120
Waves depend on the ocean water for their existence; without the ocean water, there are no waves.

91
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:06,080
At the same time, the seawater reveals itself through the form of waves.

92
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:11,420
Although the waves are constantly changing and ceasing, the true nature of the ocean water remains consistent.

93
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:18,260
Similarly, although all phenomena in the world are constantly arising and ceasing, their essential nature of emptiness remains unchanging.

94
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:25,720
This wisdom of emptiness exists not only in Mahayana Buddhism but also has unique expressions across various sects.

95
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:34,440
Zen Buddhism uses the threefold state of "seeing the mountain as a mountain, not seeing the mountain as a mountain, and seeing the mountain still as a mountain" to describe the realization and understanding of emptiness (śūnyatā).

96
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:47,300
The Pure Land School's teaching that reciting the Buddha's name even once or ten times leads to rebirth in the Pure Land reflects the transcendence of emptiness over concepts of time and number. Vajrayana's attainment of Buddhahood in one's current body exemplifies the innovative application of the wisdom of emptiness in practice methods.

97
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:55,040
The concept of "Three Thousand Realms in a Single Thought Moment," proposed by Master Zhiyi, the founder of the Tiantai School, is a theory of emptiness and a profound elucidation of the wisdom of emptiness.

98
00:10:55,240 --> 00:11:02,800
He points out that a single thought contains the three thousand worlds, the vastness is contained within the minute, and eternity is encompassed within an instant.

99
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:09,040
This is not a mystical exaggeration, but a profound insight into the non-duality of mind and the inseparability of internal and external.

100
00:11:09,320 --> 00:11:19,400
In the "Mahāprajñāpāramitāśāstra," Bodhisattva Nāgārjuna explains emptiness as follows: If a dharma exists, it is considered existent; subsequently, it will cease. If one were to assert that a dharma does not exist, then it would be regarded as permanent.

101
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:26,840
Due to the nature of emptiness, this passage reveals how emptiness transcends the two extreme views of nihilism and eternalism.

102
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:32,960
If one believes that things possess an inherent essence, then when they cease to exist, one falls into the view of annihilation.

103
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:38,040
If one believes that things do not exist inherently, one will fall into the mistaken view of eternalism and nihilism.

104
00:11:38,460 --> 00:11:45,000
The Middle Way wisdom of emptiness neither falls into existence nor non-existence, transcending these dualistic concepts.

105
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:50,080
It is important to note that understanding emptiness requires caution against two common misconceptions.

106
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:55,180
The first misconception is to understand emptiness as nihilism, the belief that nothing exists.

107
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:57,640
This is a serious distortion of the Dharma.

108
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:03,480
The Buddha clearly stated that one should neither cling to existence nor cling to non-existence.

109
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:12,100
The Middle Treatise states that one should not say "existence," nor say "non-existence," nor say "both existence and non-existence," nor say "neither existence nor non-existence." To express the whole truth, it is said as "existence."

110
00:12:12,180 --> 00:12:20,920
This passage teaches us that emptiness transcends the scope of language and concepts; any attempt to fix it with ordinary language will lead to obsession.

111
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:27,000
The second misunderstanding is to interpret emptiness as some kind of metaphysical entity or essence.

112
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:38,220
Some people, upon hearing about the emptiness of all dharmas, may imagine that there is something called "emptiness" existing either outside or within the phenomenal world. However, this precisely contradicts the true meaning of emptiness.

113
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:45,140
Emptiness is not a thing, but a description of the true nature of phenomena, characterized by the absence of an independent, permanent self-nature.

114
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:53,520
In the practice process, we may encounter such challenges: the ability to understand emptiness is a very subtle principle, yet it is difficult to truly realize and apply.

115
00:12:53,760 --> 00:13:04,860
There is a vast chasm between intellectual understanding and actual realization, much like a person who has read countless books about swimming yet has never entered the water.

116
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:11,140
True wisdom of emptiness must be experienced and verified through continuous meditation and practical living.

117
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:16,140
A breakthrough insight may help you gain a deeper understanding of emptiness.

118
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:21,700
Emptiness is not only a description of the phenomenal world but also a reflection on the way we perceive and understand it.

119
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:28,860
We are accustomed to understanding the world through concepts and language, slicing the fluid reality into fixed notions.

120
00:13:29,140 --> 00:13:35,480
However, concepts and language themselves are human constructions and cannot fully capture the entirety of reality.

121
00:13:35,720 --> 00:13:43,620
The wisdom of emptiness reminds us not to be imprisoned by our own concepts, but to remain constantly aware of the gap between conceptualization and ultimate reality.

122
00:13:43,820 --> 00:13:50,580
When we truly realize Śūnyatā (emptiness), we find that it is not a form of negative negation, but a positive form of liberation.

123
00:13:50,860 --> 00:14:00,100
It liberates us from attachment to fixed identities, roles, and concepts, allowing us to face life with greater openness and flexibility.

124
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:08,100
From the perspective of emptiness, failure is no longer the end, suffering is no longer eternal, and all experiences are processes of flow and change.

125
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:13,660
We can begin anew in every moment, having understood the profound meaning of the emptiness of all dharmas.

126
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:14,460
We can begin anew in each present moment, understanding the profound meaning of the emptiness of all phenomena.

127
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:17,780
How can we apply this wisdom to our daily lives?

128
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:23,100
Primarily, the wisdom of emptiness enables us to face change and impermanence.

129
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:31,660
When we deeply understand that everything is in constant flux and nothing is ever permanent, we can more calmly embrace the ups and downs of life.

130
00:14:32,020 --> 00:14:39,360
Losing cherished possessions, experiencing changes in relationships, and facing the decline of health no longer cause such deep suffering.

131
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:45,860
We know that attachment is the root of suffering, and only by letting go of attachment can one attain inner peace.

132
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:51,220
Secondly, the wisdom of emptiness can help us reduce attachment to the self.

133
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:58,760
We are often bound by the concepts of "I" and "mine"—my achievements, my failures, my reputation, my status.

134
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:09,460
When we realize that the self is not a fixed and unchanging entity, but a constantly changing process, we can lighten the burden of ego-centeredness and live more freely and effortlessly.

135
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:14,760
Third, the wisdom of emptiness can help us cultivate compassion.

136
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:25,420
When we realize that all sentient beings are interdependent, without absolute boundaries or distinctions, we can transcend the duality of self and others, regarding the welfare of others as our own responsibility.

137
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:32,360
True compassion is not a condescending act of charity, but a natural response to the interdependent nature of life.

138
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:42,740
Fourth, the wisdom of emptiness helps us maintain a middle way approach to life, avoiding both extreme hedonism and extreme asceticism.

139
00:15:43,180 --> 00:15:50,060
Neither blindly optimistic nor passively pessimistic, one can actively engage in worldly affairs while maintaining inner detachment.

140
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:55,540
This balanced attitude towards life is the practical manifestation of the wisdom of emptiness.

141
00:15:55,820 --> 00:16:02,340
Ultimately, the wisdom of emptiness can help us cultivate mindfulness and awareness in daily life.

142
00:16:02,620 --> 00:16:14,560
When we focus on washing the dishes while washing them, and focus on walking while walking, not being pulled by memories of the past or plans for the future, we are experiencing the emptiness of the present moment in our own way.

143
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:22,700
There is no fixed and unchanging self experiencing, nor is there a fixed and unchanging object being experienced; there is only the pure flow of experience itself.

144
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:28,480
As Bodhisattva Nagarjuna states in the "Mūlamadhyamakakārikā," all dharmas come into existence due to the emptiness of inherent existence.

145
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:31,100
If there is no emptiness, then nothing can be accomplished. It should be corrected to: If there is no emptiness (śūnyatā), nothing can be accomplished.

146
00:16:31,140 --> 00:16:35,780
It is precisely because of emptiness that all phenomena in the world can arise, change, and cease.

147
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:41,460
It is precisely because of emptiness that we can transcend attachment and attain true freedom and wisdom.

148
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:50,700
Attachment to the emptiness nature of all phenomena is not merely an abstract philosophical theory but also an art of living and a completely new perspective on viewing the world.

149
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:57,840
It teaches us not to be deceived by appearances, not to be bound by concepts, and not to be troubled by attachments.

150
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:06,700
When we let go of attachment and transcend dualistic oppositions, we can experience the true nature of life: tranquility, harmony, and freedom.

151
00:17:07,119 --> 00:17:15,099
In this rapidly changing era, in this world full of uncertainties, the wisdom of the emptiness of all phenomena is especially precious.

152
00:17:15,260 --> 00:17:24,040
It can help us face change, accept impermanence, maintain inner peace amidst chaos, and find meaning in life amid turmoil.

153
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:32,620
If today's sharing has inspired you, I hope you can bring this wisdom into your life, deeply experience it with your heart, and put it into practice through your actions.

154
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:40,240
You are also welcome to share your insights and experiences in the comment section below, so that we may grow together in the wisdom of the Dharma.

